WEBVTT

00:00:06.122 --> 00:00:10.809
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Judi Dench is one of a golden generation of actors

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:13.793
who have had long and successful careers,

00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:17.034
including Maggie Smith

00:00:18.232 --> 00:00:19.660
Vanessa Redgrave

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:22.326
Ian McKellen

00:00:23.547 --> 00:00:24.760
Michael Caine

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.266
and Anthony Hopkins.

00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:37.543
She has acted in the theater, on TV, and in movies in a career of nearly 60 years.

00:00:39.383 --> 00:00:45.281
Judith Olivia Dench was born on December 9th, 1934,

00:00:45.282 --> 00:00:48.443
near York in the northern part of England.

00:00:51.097 --> 00:00:55.278
Dench’s father was a doctor, but she had connections

00:00:55.279 --> 00:00:59.497
from a very early age to the Theatre Royal in York

00:01:00.097 --> 00:01:07.057
– her father was the theater’s doctor and her mother made costumes for the theater.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.256
As a teenager, Judi Dench acted in the York Mystery Plays.

00:01:20.011 --> 00:01:23.326
These religious plays have been performed in York

00:01:23.465 --> 00:01:26.988
every four years since the fourteenth century.

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:37.075
In the late 1950s and 1960s, she acted with several important theater companies,

00:01:38.435 --> 00:01:43.771
the Old Vic, the Royal Court, and the Royal Shakespeare Company.

00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:52.724
In the 1970s and 1980s, Dench continued to work in the theater and on TV.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:02:00.193
In 1978, she acted in Shakespeare’s <i>Macbeth</i>

00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:05.318
with the actor Ian McKellen, both in the theater and on film.

00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:12.358
Many people think it was one of the best performances of the play ever.

00:02:14.391 --> 00:02:24.094
She also appeared in movies like <i>A Room with a View</i> in 1985 and Henry V in 1989.

00:02:26.515 --> 00:02:31.020
In the 1990s, her career changed with two movies.

00:02:32.153 --> 00:02:36.398
The first of these was the James Bond movie <i>GoldenEye</i>,

00:02:36.559 --> 00:02:42.318
where Dench played Bond’s boss, M, usually a part for a man.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.405
Dench was a great success in the part.

00:02:47.132 --> 00:02:50.620
She was hard, and at the same time funny.

00:02:51.106 --> 00:02:53.612
She played the part in eight movies,

00:02:53.792 --> 00:02:58.639
with both Pierce Brosnan and Daniel Craig as James Bond.

00:03:00.705 --> 00:03:07.037
The second movie that changed her career was <i>Mrs. Brown</i> in 1997.

00:03:08.396 --> 00:03:15.238
This small movie was a great success, and she was nominated for an Oscar for Best Actress.

00:03:19.547 --> 00:03:26.431
In 1998, she played Queen Elizabeth I in the comedy <i>Shakespeare in Love</i>.

00:03:27.557 --> 00:03:29.730
The movie was another hit.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:34.449
It won the 1999 Oscar for Best Film,

00:03:34.463 --> 00:03:38.699
and Judi Dench won the Oscar for Best Supporting Actress.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:45.952
She’s only in the movie for eight minutes, but, as usual, she was fantastic.